WEBVTT

00:00.420 --> 00:02.020
- [Instructor] We're
told the parabola given

00:02.020 --> 00:06.490
by y is equal to three x
squared minus six x plus one

00:06.490 --> 00:09.550
and the line given by y minus x plus one

00:09.550 --> 00:11.020
equals zero are graphed.

00:11.020 --> 00:13.010
So we can see the parabola here in red,

00:13.010 --> 00:15.000
and we can see the line here in blue.

00:15.000 --> 00:16.310
And the first thing they ask

00:16.310 --> 00:20.600
us is one intersection point
is clearly identifiable

00:20.600 --> 00:21.580
from the graph.

00:21.580 --> 00:22.413
What is it?

00:22.413 --> 00:23.570
And they want us to put it in here.

00:23.570 --> 00:25.330
This is actually a
screenshot from the exercise

00:25.330 --> 00:26.910
on Khan Academy, but I'm just going

00:26.910 --> 00:27.743
to write on it.

00:27.743 --> 00:28.620
If you were doing it on Khan Academy,

00:28.620 --> 00:29.630
you would type it in.

00:29.630 --> 00:31.340
But pause this video and
see if you can answer

00:31.340 --> 00:32.643
this first part.

00:33.810 --> 00:37.008
All right, so one intersection
point is clearly identifiable

00:37.008 --> 00:37.950
from the graph.

00:37.950 --> 00:39.670
I see two intersection points.

00:39.670 --> 00:42.330
I see that one, and I see that one there.

00:42.330 --> 00:44.580
This second one seems clearly identifiable

00:44.580 --> 00:46.364
because, when I look at the grid,

00:46.364 --> 00:49.350
it looks clearly to be at a value

00:49.350 --> 00:51.830
of x equals two and y equals one.

00:51.830 --> 00:54.570
It seems to be the point two comma one.

00:54.570 --> 00:56.790
So it's two comma one there.

00:56.790 --> 01:00.000
And what's interesting about
these intersection points is,

01:00.000 --> 01:02.730
because it's a point
that sits on the graph

01:02.730 --> 01:06.520
of both of these curves, that means

01:06.520 --> 01:09.070
that it satisfies both of these equations,

01:09.070 --> 01:12.520
that it's a solution to
both of these equations.

01:12.520 --> 01:14.860
So the other one is find the
other intersection point.

01:14.860 --> 01:16.360
Your answer must be exact.

01:16.360 --> 01:19.120
So they want us to figure
out this intersection point

01:19.120 --> 01:20.300
right over here.

01:20.300 --> 01:22.170
Well to do that, we're going to have

01:22.170 --> 01:24.730
to try to solve this system of equations,

01:24.730 --> 01:26.630
and this is interesting
because this is a system

01:26.630 --> 01:29.590
of equations where one of
the equations is not linear,

01:29.590 --> 01:31.460
where it is a quadratic.

01:31.460 --> 01:33.470
So let's see how we could
go about doing that.

01:33.470 --> 01:34.840
So let me write down the equations.

01:34.840 --> 01:39.840
I have y is equal to three x
squared minus six x plus one.

01:41.040 --> 01:42.627
And our next equation right over here,

01:42.627 --> 01:47.627
y minus x plus one is equal to zero.

01:49.650 --> 01:51.820
Well one way to tackle,
and this is one way

01:51.820 --> 01:55.030
to tackle any system of equations,
is through substitution.

01:55.030 --> 01:58.920
So if I can rewrite
this linear equation as

01:58.920 --> 02:01.480
in terms of y, if I can solve for y,

02:01.480 --> 02:03.630
then I can substitute what y equals back

02:03.630 --> 02:06.750
into my first equation,
into my quadratic one,

02:06.750 --> 02:09.100
and then hopefully I can solve for x.

02:09.100 --> 02:10.900
So let's solve for y here.

02:10.900 --> 02:11.944
And actually, let me color code it,

02:11.944 --> 02:16.944
because this one is in red,
and this one is the line

02:18.340 --> 02:20.220
in that blue color.

02:20.220 --> 02:21.460
So let's just solve for y.

02:21.460 --> 02:25.510
The easiest way to solve for
y is to add x to both sides

02:26.530 --> 02:29.220
and subtract one from both sides.

02:29.220 --> 02:30.800
That was hard to see.

02:30.800 --> 02:33.370
So and subtract one from both sides.

02:33.370 --> 02:36.710
And so we are going to get y,

02:36.710 --> 02:38.630
and then all the rest of
the stuff cancels out,

02:38.630 --> 02:42.380
is equal to x minus one.

02:42.380 --> 02:45.380
And so now we can
substitute x minus one back

02:45.380 --> 02:50.380
in for y, and so we get
x minus one is equal

02:50.420 --> 02:55.420
to three x squared minus six x plus one.

02:56.560 --> 02:58.980
Now we wanna get a zero one side

02:58.980 --> 03:01.581
of this equation, so let's subtract x.

03:01.581 --> 03:04.020
I'll do this in a neutral color now.

03:04.020 --> 03:06.530
Let's subtract x from both sides.

03:06.530 --> 03:09.260
And let's add one to both sides.

03:09.260 --> 03:11.030
And then whatta we get?

03:11.030 --> 03:14.110
On the left-hand side we just get zero.

03:14.110 --> 03:17.320
And on the right-hand side
we get three x squared

03:17.320 --> 03:20.300
minus seven x plus two.

03:20.300 --> 03:24.280
So this is equal to zero.

03:24.280 --> 03:27.530
Now we could try to factor this.

03:27.530 --> 03:29.950
Let's see, is there an
obvious way to factor it?

03:29.950 --> 03:33.840
Can I think of two numbers, a times b,

03:33.840 --> 03:37.070
that's equal to the
product of three and two?

03:37.070 --> 03:39.200
Three times two, and if
this looks unfamiliar,

03:39.200 --> 03:41.060
you can review factoring by grouping.

03:41.060 --> 03:43.470
And can I think of those same two,

03:43.470 --> 03:45.070
a plus b, where it's going to be equal

03:45.070 --> 03:46.350
to negative seven?

03:46.350 --> 03:50.690
And actually, negative
six and negative one work.

03:50.690 --> 03:54.040
So what can do is I can
rewrite this whole thing

03:54.040 --> 03:57.230
as zero is equal to three x squared,

03:57.230 --> 03:59.910
and then instead of negative seven x,

03:59.910 --> 04:04.180
I can write negative six x and minus x.

04:04.180 --> 04:05.890
And then I have my plus two.

04:05.890 --> 04:07.310
I'm just factoring by grouping,

04:07.310 --> 04:09.150
for those of you are not
familiar with this technique.

04:09.150 --> 04:11.230
You could also use the quadratic formula.

04:11.230 --> 04:13.500
So then zero is equal to,

04:13.500 --> 04:17.680
so if I group these first two I can factor

04:17.680 --> 04:18.640
out a three x.

04:18.640 --> 04:23.470
So I'm going to get three
x times x minus two,

04:23.470 --> 04:28.250
and in these second two I can factor out.

04:28.250 --> 04:30.920
In these second two I can
factor out a negative one,

04:30.920 --> 04:34.360
so I have negative one times x minus two.

04:34.360 --> 04:36.430
And then I can factor out a negative two.

04:36.430 --> 04:39.030
I'll scroll down a little
bit so I have some space.

04:39.030 --> 04:42.810
So I have zero is equal to,

04:42.810 --> 04:45.690
if I factor out an x minus two,

04:45.690 --> 04:50.690
I'm going to get x minus
two times three x minus one.

04:51.740 --> 04:54.370
And so a solution would be a situation

04:54.370 --> 04:56.450
where either of these is equal to zero,

04:56.450 --> 04:58.650
or, I'll scroll down a little bit more.

04:58.650 --> 05:01.430
So x minus two could be equal to zero,

05:01.430 --> 05:04.760
or three x minus one is equal to zero.

05:04.760 --> 05:06.700
The point at which x
minus two equals zero is

05:06.700 --> 05:08.660
when x is equal to two.

05:08.660 --> 05:10.470
And for three x minus one equals zero,

05:10.470 --> 05:11.510
add one to both sides.

05:11.510 --> 05:13.310
You get three x is equal to one,

05:13.310 --> 05:15.440
or x is equal to 1/3.

05:15.440 --> 05:17.190
So we figured out the,

05:17.190 --> 05:20.050
we already saw the solution
where x equals two.

05:20.050 --> 05:21.590
That's this point right over here.

05:21.590 --> 05:23.200
We already typed that in.

05:23.200 --> 05:24.570
But now we figured out the x value

05:24.570 --> 05:25.510
of the other solution.

05:25.510 --> 05:28.147
So this is x is equal
to 1/3 right over here.

05:28.147 --> 05:30.230
So our x value is 1/3.

05:30.230 --> 05:32.210
But we still have to
figure out the y value.

05:32.210 --> 05:34.300
Well the y value's going
to be the corresponding y

05:34.300 --> 05:37.670
we get for that x in either equation.

05:37.670 --> 05:39.960
And I like to focus on the simpler

05:39.960 --> 05:41.640
of the two equations.

05:41.640 --> 05:44.760
So we could figure out what is x when,

05:44.760 --> 05:48.640
or what is y when x is equal
to 1/3 using this equation?

05:48.640 --> 05:49.590
We could have used the original one,

05:49.590 --> 05:50.730
but this is even simpler.

05:50.730 --> 05:52.460
It's already solved for y.

05:52.460 --> 05:55.620
So y is equal to 1/3 minus one.

05:55.620 --> 05:58.010
I'm just substituting
that 1/3 back into this.

05:58.010 --> 06:01.220
And so you get y is equal to negative 2/3.

06:01.220 --> 06:02.420
And it looks like that as well.

06:02.420 --> 06:05.580
y is equal to negative
2/3 right over there.

06:05.580 --> 06:10.500
So is the point 1/3 comma negative 2/3.

06:10.500 --> 06:11.553
And we're done.
